import itertools

def count_equivariant(n, p):
    count = 0
    for a in itertools.product(range(len(p)), repeat=n):
        if all(a[(k + 1) % n] == p[a[k]] for k in range(n)):
            count += 1
    return count
```

Out of $m^n$ candidate functions, exactly $m$ are equivariant: each is determined by $a[0]$ (the image of the identity element), since then $a[k] = p^k(a[0])$ — and any choice of $a[0]$ works because $p^n = \mathrm{id}$. This is the Yoneda lemma for the one-object category of $\Z/n$.
